ed by the revolutionary ideals of the French Revolution of 1789."}, {"sourceSlug": "socialist-international", "sourceName": "Socialist International", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "french-communist-party", "targetName": "French Communist Party", "context": "The French Communist Party collaborated with the Socialist International to promote leftist policies on a global scale."}], "places": [{"name": "Saint-Denis, France", "role": "Headquarters"}, {"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Major political center"}, {"name": "Algiers, Algeria", "role": "Anti-colonial struggle"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: French Communist Party", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "french-communist-party"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q192821", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Communist_Party"], "tags": ["France", "political party", "Europe", "Political Parties & Organizations", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "French Communist Party left a notable mark on Modern history, documented across 60 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The French Communist Party played a crucial role in shaping labor rights and leftist politics in France, influencing the social fabric of the nation and impacting anti-colonial movements, particularly in Algeria. Its legacy continues to resonate in contemporary French political discourse.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-14T06:13:39Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27490245124", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "French political party Headquartered in Headquarters of the French Communist Party. Founded 1920.", "newValue": "The French Communist Party (Parti communiste français, PCF), established in 1920, is a major political institution in France that emerged in the aftermath of World War I and the Russian Revolution. Its foundation marked a significant shift in the French political landscape, embodying the aspirations of the working class and the leftist movements of the time. Key figures such as Maurice Thorez and Georges Marchais played vital roles in the party’s development, which was headquartered in the historic suburb of Saint-Denis, Paris. The PCF's influence extended beyond France, as it became a vital component of the international communist movement, aligning itself with the Soviet Union during the Cold War era.

